```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "search",
      "fault_kind": "pod_failure",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "metric",
          "sql": "SELECT 'a' p, attr_k8s_pod_name pod, metric, count(*) n, min(value) minv, max(value) maxv FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE metric IN ('k8s.container.restarts') AND attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'search%' GROUP BY ALL UNION ALL SELECT 'n', attr_k8s_pod_name, metric, count(*), min(value), max(value) FROM normal_metrics WHERE metric IN ('k8s.container.restarts') AND attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'search%' GROUP BY ALL",
          "claim": "Search pod restart counter rose from 0 to 3-6 during the incident; it was 0 throughout the normal period."
        },
        {
          "kind": "metric",
          "sql": "SELECT 'a' p, metric, attr_k8s_pod_name pod, round(avg(value),3) avgv, round(max(value),3) maxv FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'search%' AND metric IN ('k8s.container.ready','container.cpu.usage','container.memory.working_set') GROUP BY ALL UNION ALL SELECT 'n', metric, attr_k8s_pod_name, round(avg(value),3), round(max(value),3) FROM normal_metrics WHERE attr_k8s_pod_name LIKE 'search%' AND metric IN ('k8s.container.ready','container.cpu.usage','container.memory.working_set') GROUP BY ALL",
          "claim": "Search container readiness fell to 0 on average while CPU and memory stayed at normal levels, so this is a restart loop, not resource saturation."
        },
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, attr_status_code, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,3) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE span_name LIKE 'search.Search/Nearby%' OR service_name='search' GROUP BY ALL ORDER BY 1,2,3",
          "claim": "In the incident search emits no server spans at all; frontend's search.Search/Nearby client calls all return Error in about 0.02 ms."
        },
        {
          "kind": "log",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, level, substr(message,1,80) msg, count(*) n FROM abnormal_logs WHERE service_name='frontend' AND level='ERROR' GROUP BY ALL",
          "claim": "Frontend logs 1128 ERROR lines 'Search service failed' during the incident, with no search-service logs at all."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {
      "from": "search",
      "to": "frontend",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, attr_http_response_status_code code, attr_status_code st, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE (span_name='search.Search/Nearby' AND attr_span_kind='Client') OR span_name='HTTP /hotels' GROUP BY ALL ORDER BY 1,2,3",
          "claim": "Every failed search.Search/Nearby client call in frontend coincides with an HTTP /hotels server span returning 500, 780 of each."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The user-visible degradation traces to one fault: the search service crash-looping, which is classified as `pod_failure` because it restarts and recovers within the window. Its pod's restart counter rose from 0 to 3–6 during the incident. Its readiness dropped to zero on average, and CPU and memory stayed normal, so this is not resource exhaustion. Search emits no spans or logs during the incident. Every frontend call to `search.Search/Nearby` fails within about 0.02 ms, and the `/hotels` user endpoint returns HTTP 500 for each of those 780 requests. Frontend logs 1,128 "Search service failed" errors over the same period.

The other services that dropped off are downstream symptoms, not separate faults. Geo and rate are only called by search, so their spans disappear with it. The reservation calls made as part of `/hotels` are skipped when search fails. Hubble network metrics show no anomaly on search or frontend connections. Recommendation's restart counter is flat at 7 across the window, so it is not part of this incident. The `pod_failure` label could be `pod_unavailable` under the collapsed equivalence class, since the telemetry does not show whether the instance recovers before the window ends.
